-- title:
--   The $h^{-2d}$ normalisation cancels the two cube volumes
-- statement:
--   Let $d \in \mathbb{N}$ and let $h \in \mathbb{R}$ with $h > 0$. In the extended nonnegative reals $\overline{\mathbb{R}}_{\ge 0} = [0,\infty]$,
--   $$\iota\bigl((h^{2d})^{-1}\bigr)\cdot\bigl(\iota(h^{d})\cdot \iota(h^{d})\bigr) \;=\; 1 ,$$
--   where $\iota(r)$ denotes the image of a real number $r$ in $[0,\infty]$ (the value being $0$ for $r \le 0$) and all powers are natural-number powers.
--
--   The identity is the bookkeeping behind the discretized kernel: the prefactor $h^{-2d}$ in
--   $\omega^k_h(x,y) = h^{-2d}\iint_{A_h(x)\times A_h(y)} k$ exactly cancels the product of the two cube volumes $h^d$, so that averaging over a pair of cubes is a genuine mean and the constants in the resulting estimates do not depend on the scale $h$.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The statement lives in $\overline{\mathbb{R}}_{\ge 0}$, where multiplication is not cancellative and $\iota$ is not a ring homomorphism on all of $\mathbb{R}$; the positivity hypothesis $h > 0$ is what makes the three factors finite and nonzero, so the cancellation is legitimate.
